TITLE:: SDGSamplePlayer
summary:: one way of reading a sample buffer, registered with a SuperDirtGUI
categories:: Libraries>SuperDirtGUI
related:: Classes/SuperDirtGUI, Classes/SuperDirt, Classes/DirtModule

DESCRIPTION::
A sample event needs a SynthDef, and the right one depends on two things: how
the sample should be read - warped, granular, backwards - and how many channels
the buffer has. This class holds one way of reading: a name, a test that says
when it wants the event, and the prefix its SynthDef names are built from.
link::Classes/SuperDirtGUI:: keeps them in an ordered list, and its player
module - in front of SuperDirt's own code::sound:: module - asks that list on
every sample event. When no player wants the event, SuperDirt plays it the way
it always has.

A player is made with link::Classes/SuperDirtGUI#-addSamplePlayer::, usually
from a file in code::players/::, and is named after the Tidal parameter that
switches it on, like a module:

code::
(
~sdg.addSamplePlayer(\sdggrain, { |sampleNumChannels, numChannels|
	{ |out, bufnum, sustain = 1, begin = 0, end = 1, speed = 1, endSpeed = 1, freq = 440, pan = 0,
		sdgdensity = 40, sdggrain = 0.1|
		var rate = SDGBufRate.kr(speed, endSpeed, sustain, freq);
		var phase = SDGBufPhase.ar(bufnum, rate, begin, end, speed);
		var sound = GrainBuf.ar(1, Impulse.ar(sdgdensity), sdggrain, bufnum, rate, phase / max(BufFrames.ir(bufnum), 1), 4);
		Out.ar(out, DirtPan.ar(sound, numChannels, pan))
	}
}, { ~sdggrain.notNil });
)

~dirt.orbits[0].valuePairs([\s, \bd, \sdggrain, 0.1]);          // chosen by its test
~dirt.orbits[0].valuePairs([\s, \bd, \player, \sdggrain]);      // chosen by name
::

From Tidal that is code::# pF "sdggrain" 0.1:: and code::# pS "player" "sdggrain"::.

subsection:: The SynthDef function

code::synthFunc:: is called once per possible sample channel count, from 1 to
code::SuperDirt.maxSampleNumChannels::, with code::(sampleNumChannels,
numChannels)::, and answers the SynthDef function; one SynthDef named
code::<defPrefix>_<sampleChannels>_<numChannels>:: is built for each. Keep it
pure. Two rules for the function it answers:

list::
## the nine standard controls come first, in this order: code::out, bufnum,
sustain, begin, end, speed, endSpeed, freq, pan::. link::#*defaultRates:: maps
onto them positionally - all code::\ir::, code::pan:: code::\kr::.
## anything declared after them is control rate, is sent only when the event
carries a value for it, and can be set or bus-mapped while the synth runs.
::

No argument list is written by hand. The player module reads the control
names from the SynthDesc and sends every one the event has a non-nil value
for, plus the buffer under code::bufnum:: and code::freq:: tuned by the
sample's metadata - exactly what SuperDirt's own code::sound:: module sends
to code::dirt_sample::. code::SDGBufRate:: and code::SDGBufPhase:: give the
playback rate and the begin-to-end read pointer code::dirt_sample:: uses, so
the trim, code::speed::, code::accelerate:: and code::n:: mean what they
always mean.

subsection:: Prepare

An optional function, run inside the event just before its arguments are read,
for what a SynthDef cannot do alone: change code::~begin:: or code::~sustain::,
or look up a second buffer. It is called with the code::DirtEvent::. SuperDirt
has worked out code::~sustain:: by then but has not sent it, so what prepare
leaves there is what the event lasts. This is the one way past SuperDirt's cap:
code::sustain:: and code::legato:: stop at the length of the sample.

code::
(
~sdg.addSamplePlayer(\sdglong, { |sampleNumChannels, numChannels|
	{ |out, bufnum, sustain = 1, begin = 0, end = 1, speed = 1, endSpeed = 1, freq = 440, pan = 0|
		var rate = SDGBufRate.kr(speed, endSpeed, sustain, freq);
		var phase = SDGBufPhase.ar(bufnum, rate, begin, end, speed);
		Out.ar(out, DirtPan.ar(BufRd.ar(sampleNumChannels, bufnum, phase, 0, 4), numChannels, pan))
	}
}, { ~sdglong.notNil }, prepare: { ~sustain = ~sdglong / ~cps }); // sdglong cycles long
)
::

code::players/sdgfreeze.scd:: lengthens an event like this,
code::players/sdgonset.scd:: moves code::~begin:: and code::~end::, and
code::players/sdgcross.scd:: looks up a second buffer.

subsection:: Selection

An explicit code::~player:: in the event wins. Otherwise the first player in
the list whose test passes is used; code::addSamplePlayer:: puts a new player
at the front, so the most recently added one takes precedence. A player is
its name: adding one with the same name replaces it in place.


CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
Make a player; link::Classes/SuperDirtGUI#-addSamplePlayer:: does this and
builds the SynthDefs.
ARGUMENT:: name
the Tidal parameter that switches it on.
ARGUMENT:: synthFunc
the function described above; nil for a player whose SynthDefs are defined
elsewhere.
ARGUMENT:: test
a function evaluated inside the event; true when nil.
ARGUMENT:: defPrefix
the SynthDef name prefix; the name when nil.
ARGUMENT:: rates
the rates of the nine standard controls; link::#*defaultRates:: when nil.
ARGUMENT:: prepare
an optional function run inside the event before its arguments are read; see link::#Prepare::.

METHOD:: defaultRates
code::[\ir, \ir, \ir, \ir, \ir, \ir, \ir, \ir, \kr]::.


INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: name
The name, a Symbol.

METHOD:: test
METHOD:: synthFunc
METHOD:: defPrefix
METHOD:: rates
As given.

METHOD:: prepare
As given; see link::#Prepare::.

METHOD:: defName
The SynthDef for a buffer with that many channels, played into that many.

METHOD:: instrumentFor
The SynthDef for an event: the buffer event carries code::bufNumChannels::,
the orbit's default event carries code::numChannels::.

METHOD:: matches
Whether the player wants the event: the test, run inside it.

METHOD:: makeSynthDefs
Build and add one SynthDef per sample channel count for this many output
channels. Nothing happens for a player without code::synthFunc::.
